// The two ways a value turns into text.
//
// `Display` is the text a value has for a reader: a number is its digits, a string is its characters, and nothing is
// quoted or escaped. `Debug` is the text a value has for whoever is looking at a program: a string is quoted with
// its escapes visible, a character is quoted, and a structure shows its parts. They differ for exactly the types
// where the difference matters, and a type with only one sensible rendering implements both the same way.
//
// Both write into a `Writer` rather than answering with a `String`, which is what makes printing a value allocate
// nothing. Rendering to a string is then one writer among several.
//
// Neither takes a specification. Width, alignment, precision and base belong to the placeholder rather than the
// value, and are applied by what drives the writer; a type that implemented them itself would have to reimplement
// them, and would get them subtly different.

import Text::TextError;

/// The text a value has for a reader.
///
/// https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/format/display
interface Display {
    /// Writes this value's text, reporting what stopped it.
    func Format(writer: Writer) -> TextError;
}

/// The text a value has for whoever is inspecting a program.
///
/// https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/format/debug
interface Debug {
    /// Writes this value's inspection text, reporting what stopped it.
    func FormatDebug(writer: Writer) -> TextError;
}
